/-!
# Bundles over `X × ℂP¹` are clutchings; the clutched lines `L m`; linear clutchings

Let `X` be compact Hausdorff and `F` a finite-rank core over `X × ℙ ℂ (Fin 2 → ℂ)`, and let `E` be
its restriction to `X × {[1 : 1]}`, that is its pullback along `x ↦ (x, [1 : 1])`. Then `F` is
isomorphic to the clutching `E.clutching f` of `E` by some automorphism `f` of the pullback of `E`
to `X × Circle` (`TauCeti.FiniteRankBundleCore.exists_iso_clutching`), and over `ℂ` the
automorphism may be taken a Laurent polynomial in the circle coordinate
(`TauCeti.FiniteRankBundleCore.exists_iso_clutching_laurent`). These are the first steps of the
product theorem of [Hatcher2017, §2.1].

The file also treats the clutched lines `L m = (trivial ℂ X 1).clutching (circleZPowIso X m)`,
clutched by `u ↦ u ^ m`, for `X` paracompact and R₁: `L m ⊕ L n ≅ L (m + n) ⊕ L 0`, hence in
`K(X × ℂP¹)` the class of `L m` is `1 + m • (1 - [H])`, with `H = L (-1)` the pullback of the
tautological line, and in particular `[H⁻¹] = [L 1] = 2 - [H]`.

Last, for `X` paracompact and R₁, a clutching function `u + T` that is linear in the circle
coordinate `u`, with `T` an endomorphism of `E` with no eigenvalue on the unit circle, splits
along the Riesz projection of `T`: the part where the eigenvalues lie in the unit disc is twisted
by `L 1`, and the rest is untwisted, as in [Hatcher2017, §2.1].

## Main definitions and results

* `TauCeti.FiniteRankBundleCore.nonempty_pullback_affineChartMap_iso`: over `X × (Fin 1 → ℂ)`,
  the pullback of `F` along the inverse `ProjectiveLine.affineChartMap X i` of an affine chart of
  the projective line is the pullback of `E` along the projection, since the affine line
  contracts to the coordinate `1` of `[1 : 1]`
  (`TauCeti.FiniteRankBundleCore.nonempty_iso_pullback_fst`). Here `E` is the pullback of `F`
  along `ProjectiveLine.prodMkOne X`.
* `TauCeti.FiniteRankBundleCore.nonempty_pullback_subtypeVal_iso_of_subset_affineOpen`: hence the
  same holds for the restriction of `F` to `X × S`, for any set `S` inside an affine open.
* `TauCeti.FiniteRankBundleCore.exists_iso_clutching`: every core over `X × ℙ ℂ (Fin 2 → ℂ)` is a
  clutching of its restriction to `X × {[1 : 1]}`.
* `TauCeti.FiniteRankBundleCore.exists_iso_clutching_laurent`: for a complex core, the clutching
  function may be taken a Laurent family `laurentHom N a`, with fibre map
  `∑_{|i| ≤ N} u ^ i • (a i).fiberMap x` at `(x, u)`.
* `TauCeti.FiniteRankBundleCore.whitneySum_clutching_circleZPowIso`: `L m ⊕ L n ≅ L (m + n) ⊕ L 0`.
* `TauCeti.SplitK0.of_clutching_circleZPowIso`: the class of `L m` is `1 + m • (1 - [H])`;
  `of_clutching_circleZPowIso_zero` and `of_clutching_circleZPowIso_neg_one` are the cases
  `m = 0` and `m = -1`, which need no hypothesis on `X`.
* `TauCeti.SplitK0.of_clutching_circleZPowIso_one`: `[H⁻¹] = 2 - [H]`.
* `TauCeti.SplitK0.of_clutching_circleZPowIso_mem_closure`: the class of every `L m` lies in the
  additive subgroup generated by `1` and `[H]`.
* `TauCeti.FiniteRankBundleCore.clutching_smulIdempotentAddIso`: for an idempotent `P` of a
  complex core `E` with `P` and `1 - P` of constant rank, the clutching of `E` by
  `u P + (1 - P)` (`TauCeti.FiniteRankBundleCore.smulIdempotentAddIso`) is the Whitney sum of
  the pullback of the range of `P` twisted by `L 1` and the pullback of the range of `1 - P`.
* `TauCeti.FiniteRankBundleCore.clutching_smul_add`: for an endomorphism `T` of a complex core `E`
  with no eigenvalue of norm `1` on any fibre, and `P` its Riesz projection, the clutching of `E`
  by `u + T` is the Whitney sum of the pullback of the range of `P` twisted by `L 1` and the
  pullback of the range of `1 - P`.
* `TauCeti.FiniteRankBundleCore.exists_nonempty_clutching_iso_whitneySum_of_linear`: over a
  compact Hausdorff preconnected base, the clutching by any linear family `u a + b` is, up to
  isomorphism, of the same form, `E₁ ⊗ L 1 ⊕ E₂` on pullbacks of two cores `E₁` and `E₂`.

The discs, the annulus and the maps between products with them that the proof uses, such as
`ProjectiveLine.circleToAnnulus` and the radial deformation `ProjectiveLine.annulusDeformation`,
are in `SymplecticGeometry.Topology.ComplexProjectiveSpace.ProjectiveLine`.

## Proof

Each of the two discs lies in an affine open, so the restriction of `F` to its product with `X` is
the pullback of `E` along the projection, by homotopy invariance of pullbacks applied to the
segments to `[1 : 1]` in the affine chart
(`TauCeti.FiniteRankBundleCore.nonempty_iso_pullback_fst`). By
`TauCeti.FiniteRankBundleCore.isoGlue`, `F` is then the gluing of the pullback of `E` by the
transition `g` of the two isomorphisms over the product of `X` with the annulus. Let `f` be `g`
restricted to `X × Circle`. The radial deformation of the annulus onto the circle joins `g` to
`clutchingAut E f`, which glues to `E.clutching f`, and gluings by homotopic automorphisms are
isomorphic (`TauCeti.FiniteRankBundleCore.nonempty_iso_glue_of_homotopic`).

For a complex core, `TauCeti.FiniteRankBundleCore.exists_iso_clutching_laurentHom` then replaces
`f` by a Laurent family whose clutching is isomorphic to that by `f`.

For the lines, `L m ⊕ L n` and `L (m + n) ⊕ L 0` are the clutchings of the sum of two trivial
lines by `diag (u ^ m, u ^ n)` and `diag (u ^ (m + n), 1)`
(`TauCeti.FiniteRankBundleCore.clutching_whitneySum`). These are joined in `GL₂(ℂ)` by
`R_t⁻¹ · diag (1, u ^ n) · R_t · diag (u ^ m, 1)`, with `R_t` the rotation by `π t / 2`, which is
the quarter turn `(v, w) ↦ (-w, v)` at `t = 1`. The family is assembled from morphisms of cores:
`R_t` is `TauCeti.FiniteRankBundleCore.whitneyRotation` with the functions `cos (π t / 2)` and
`sin (π t / 2)`, and the diagonal factors are Whitney sums of pullbacks of `circleZPowIso`. So the
clutchings are isomorphic
(`TauCeti.FiniteRankBundleCore.nonempty_iso_clutching_of_homotopic`). Taking `n = 1` and
`n = -1` gives the class of `L m` by induction on `m`, from `L 0 ≅ 1`
(`TauCeti.FiniteRankBundleCore.clutching_one`) and `L (-1) ≅ H`
(`TauCeti.FiniteRankBundleCore.clutching_z`).

For the clutching by `u + T`, let `P` be the Riesz projection of `T`, which commutes with `T`.
The segment from `u + T` to `(u P + (1 - P)) (P + T (1 - P))` is, at time `t`, the map
`(u + t T) P + (t u + T) (1 - P)`, which is invertible on each fibre since the eigenvalues of `T`
lie inside the unit disc on the range of `P` and outside it on the kernel
(`TauCeti.FiniteRankBundleCore.disjoint_ker_id_sub_smul_range_unitDiscRieszProjection`,
`TauCeti.FiniteRankBundleCore.disjoint_ker_sub_smul_ker_unitDiscRieszProjection`). The constant
factor `P + T (1 - P)`, an automorphism of `E`, does not change the clutching
(`TauCeti.FiniteRankBundleCore.nonempty_iso_clutching_pullback_trans`), and the splitting
`TauCeti.FiniteRankBundleCore.rangeWhitneySumIso` carries `u P + (1 - P)` to the block-diagonal
family `u ⊕ 1`, whose clutching is the Whitney sum above, for any idempotent `P` of constant
rank (`TauCeti.FiniteRankBundleCore.clutching_smulIdempotentAddIso`,
`TauCeti.FiniteRankBundleCore.clutching_whitneySum`,
`TauCeti.FiniteRankBundleCore.clutching_circleZPowSMul`).

For a general linear family `u a + b`, whose leading coefficient need not be invertible,
`TauCeti.FiniteRankBundleCore.exists_isIso_nonempty_iso_clutching_linear` first replaces it by
`u A + B` with `A` invertible. Composing with the pullback of `A⁻¹` does not change the clutching
(`TauCeti.FiniteRankBundleCore.nonempty_iso_clutching_trans_pullback`) and gives `u + A⁻¹ B`.
Since this is invertible at every point of the circle, `A⁻¹ B` has no eigenvalue of norm `1`
(`TauCeti.FiniteRankBundleCore.norm_ne_one_of_mem_spectrum_fiberMap`), and the case
above applies, the ranks being constant over a preconnected base.

## References

* [A. Hatcher, *Vector Bundles and K-Theory*][Hatcher2017], §2.1 (every bundle over `X × S²` is a
  clutching; the relation `[H⁻¹] = 2 - [H]` and the rotation that proves it; linear clutching
  functions).
* T. Kato, *Perturbation Theory for Linear Operators*, Springer, Chapter I, §4.6 (Riesz
  projections).
-/

